{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EqaQSRfGuupEH8DSVHeyRb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Capability catalog\n2\t\n3\tThe capability adapter is used by clients which discover operations by capability\n4\tname rather than by a dedicated tool. It shares the same identity and Store with\n5\tthe dedicated routes. Metadata supplies the dispatch operation, required scope\n6\tand owner-only policy. Adding catalog entries requires a matching dispatch arm.\n7\t\n8\t`document.preview` returns a complete document, including its body. It is a\n9\tconvenience name for read access, not a public redacted summary. `account.archive`\n10\treturns all account rows for one tenant, equivalent to account.export. The optional\n11\ttenant parameter supports clients that always include their active workspace.\n12\tIt is a selector, not a delegation mechanism. Omitting it selects the caller's\n13\town tenant. Capability jobs are created anew on every request and are not cached.\n14\t\n15\tMissing capability names are invalid requests. Missing preview keys are invalid\n16\trequests. Unknown storage keys are NotFound. No route permits tenant discovery,\n17\tbulk scope changes, session creation, or attaching arbitrary Permit objects.\n18\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5430cdda-5373-4192-a860-e85bfed12d9e","uuid":"c12ab28c-78f9-4495-a0cf-e8c6a345c632","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:50:25.008Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/docs/capabilities.md","content":"# Capability catalog\n\nThe capability adapter is used by clients which discover operations by capability\nname rather than by a dedicated tool. It shares the same identity and Store with\nthe dedicated routes. Metadata supplies the dispatch operation, required scope\nand owner-only policy. Adding catalog entries requires a matching dispatch arm.\n\n`document.preview` returns a complete document, including its body. It is a\nconvenience name for read access, not a public redacted summary. `account.archive`\nreturns all account rows for one tenant, equivalent to account.export. The optional\ntenant parameter supports clients that always include their active workspace.\nIt is a selector, not a delegation mechanism. Omitting it selects the caller's\nown tenant. Capability jobs are created anew on every request and are not cached.\n\nMissing capability names are invalid requests. Missing preview keys are invalid\nrequests. Unknown storage keys are NotFound. No route permits tenant discovery,\nbulk scope changes, session creation, or attaching arbitrary Permit objects.\n","numLines":18,"startLine":1,"totalLines":18}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EqaQSRfGuupEH8DSVHeyRb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BurcJDatZFgs2v1P2iyMW3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Request lifecycle and operational decisions\n2\t\n3\t## Construction\n4\t\n5\tThe host calls the factory once per isolated workspace. The factory validates the\n6\tlocal Settings instance, constructs memory storage and an audit queue, and binds\n7\troute methods to the registry. The host must retain that returned registry for\n8\tchanges and events to persist within its process. Creating a second registry\n9\tcreates independent storage and handlers. A request cannot select a factory or\n10\treplace a handler through an argument field.\n11\t\n12\t## Admission\n13\t\n14\tRegistry.invoke first verifies the argument container and named handler. It then\n15\tlooks up the token in the synthetic session table and rejects an inactive identity.\n16\tThe immutable identity is attached to Request. Extra argument properties are not\n17\tcopied into the identity. A request ID is useful for correlating successful events\n18\tbut is not interpreted as a role, tenant, permission, grant, or resource name.\n19\tAn authenticated request is still subject to the route's resource policy.\n20\t\n21\t## Planning and data\n22\t\n23\tRoute helpers validate selectors before a storage call. A syntactically valid\n24\tselector can still designate data the caller is forbidden to receive. Internal\n25\tplans and jobs carry the selected resource to the final dispatch helper; dispatch\n26\treturns complete record data, not an automatically redacted representation.\n27\tConsequently, admission, validation and operation selection are separate decisions.\n28\tA host should preserve the service's typed refusal rather than retrying a failed\n29\toperation under an owner token. No such retry exists in the supplied dispatcher.\n30\t\n31\t## Completion\n32\t\n33\tSuccessful record responses are materialized in memory. Audit recording follows\n34\tsuccessful dispatch. An exception prevents the success event from being appended;\n35\tthis lightweight queue is therefore not a complete ledger of denied attempts.\n36\tThe queue records no payload body and is not consulted for authorization decisions.\n37\tThe response limit is a construction-time sanity bound, while batch size checking\n38\toccurs at the argument boundary. Neither is a substitute for record permissions.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope of this source bundle\n41\t\n42\tThe TOML files document the shipped local profile. They are not hot-reloaded and\n43\tcannot change handler registration. The Python Settings object is the executable\n44\tsource of defaults. No deployment manifest or installed host plugin is provided.\n45\tThe behavior of a future network host, tenant federation, storage persistence or\n46\tcredential rotation is unknown. Reviewers should distinguish those unknowns from\n47\tconcrete paths implemented in this source tree. Synthetic fixtures should never be\n48\tused as live credentials or treated as a production identity provisioning system.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5430cdda-5373-4192-a860-e85bfed12d9e","uuid":"ae1c792b-7247-4625-8eec-74bb0c2ab2cf","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:50:25.011Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/docs/request-lifecycle.md","content":"# Request lifecycle and operational decisions\n\n## Construction\n\nThe host calls the factory once per isolated workspace. The factory validates the\nlocal Settings instance, constructs memory storage and an audit queue, and binds\nroute methods to the registry. The host must retain that returned registry for\nchanges and events to persist within its process. Creating a second registry\ncreates independent storage and handlers. A request cannot select a factory or\nreplace a handler through an argument field.\n\n## Admission\n\nRegistry.invoke first verifies the argument container and named handler. It then\nlooks up the token in the synthetic session table and rejects an inactive identity.\nThe immutable identity is attached to Request. Extra argument properties are not\ncopied into the identity. A request ID is useful for correlating successful events\nbut is not interpreted as a role, tenant, permission, grant, or resource name.\nAn authenticated request is still subject to the route's resource policy.\n\n## Planning and data\n\nRoute helpers validate selectors before a storage call. A syntactically valid\nselector can still designate data the caller is forbidden to receive. Internal\nplans and jobs carry the selected resource to the final dispatch helper; dispatch\nreturns complete record data, not an automatically redacted representation.\nConsequently, admission, validation and operation selection are separate decisions.\nA host should preserve the service's typed refusal rather than retrying a failed\noperation under an owner token. No such retry exists in the supplied dispatcher.\n\n## Completion\n\nSuccessful record responses are materialized in memory. Audit recording follows\nsuccessful dispatch. An exception prevents the success event from being appended;\nthis lightweight queue is therefore not a complete ledger of denied attempts.\nThe queue records no payload body and is not consulted for authorization decisions.\nThe response limit is a construction-time sanity bound, while batch size checking\noccurs at the argument boundary. Neither is a substitute for record permissions.\n\n## Scope of this source bundle\n\nThe TOML files document the shipped local profile. They are not hot-reloaded and\ncannot change handler registration. The Python Settings object is the executable\nsource of defaults. No deployment manifest or installed host plugin is provided.\nThe behavior of a future network host, tenant federation, storage persistence or\ncredential rotation is unknown. Reviewers should distinguish those unknowns from\nconcrete paths implemented in this source tree. Synthetic fixtures should never be\nused as live credentials or treated as a production identity provisioning system.\n","numLines":49,"startLine":1,"totalLines":49}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BurcJDatZFgs2v1P2iyMW3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
